Good point, thanks. I added `test_fused_decode_feeds_zero_positions_to_kernel` in 60def01661.

It runs the real `_fused_decode` on CPU against a fake `aiter.ops.cache` and records what reaches `fused_qk_rope_concat_and_cache_mla`. The input is a CUDA-graph-padded batch, `slot_mapping = [[5], [9], [2], [-1]]`. The test asserts:
- the kernel gets the flattened `slot_mapping`, `-1` pad included;
- the positions are all zero and the same length as that `slot_mapping`;
- the positions are a view of the persistent `_zero_positions` buffer, so nothing is allocated inside a captured graph;
- the output of the stubbed `_v_up_proj` is returned.

I checked it in the `nightly-rocm100-df6603f0` image with no GPU attached:
- with this PR, the whole file passes (6 passed);
- with the real positions put back (`torch.arange(num_slots)` in place of the zero buffer), the new test fails on the all-zero assertion.

